\title{Cubical Recipe Spaces:\\Fibers, Substitution Paths, and Parity Classification}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{30 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We introduce a finite combinatorial framework for recipe variation over a fixed observable flavor profile. A recipe is decomposed into a core component and optional ingredient data. The fiber over any fixed flavor is naturally equivalent to the optional state space; consequently, one binary option yields two states and $n$ independent binary options yield the $2^n$ vertices of an $n$-cube. Substitution methods are finite lists of coordinate toggles. We prove that executing a method from a binary recipe $r$ produces $r\oplus\sigma(p)$, where $\sigma(p)$ is its parity signature. Thus two methods have the same endpoint from a fixed recipe exactly when their signatures agree. Independent substitutions form commuting squares, repeated substitutions cancel, loops are exactly zero-signature methods, and every method followed by its reversal is a loop. We give linear-time algorithms and clarify which additional structure is required for nontrivial topological conclusions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Recipes are often printed as linear instructions, but recipe variation is naturally spatial. Optional ingredients create alternative states, substitutions create transitions, and independent substitutions create squares. A mathematical treatment should distinguish an observable dish, the collection of recipes realizing it, and the methods transforming one recipe into another.

We model a flavor map as forgetting optional data. The recipes with a fixed observed flavor form a fiber. For independent binary choices, this fiber is the vertex set of a Boolean cube. A method is an edge path generated by coordinate toggles. Its endpoint is completely determined by the parity with which each coordinate occurs.

This is a finite exact model, not an empirical claim that flavor itself is binary. ``Optional'' means invisible to the declared observation, and ``independent'' means that all binary assignments and single-coordinate toggles are admitted. The results identify the consequences of those modeling choices and separate them from stronger claims about measured culinary datasets.

The principal results classify fixed-flavor recipes, count binary fibers, derive an endpoint formula, classify method endpoints, and establish square, cancellation, loop, and reversal laws. They also expose an algebraic action of $(\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z)^n$ and provide efficient computational procedures.

\section{Flavor fibers}

Let $C$ be a set of observable cores and $O$ a set of optional ingredient states.

\begin{definition}[Recipe and flavor]
A recipe is a pair $(c,o)\in C\times O$. Its flavor is the projection
\[
F(c,o)=c.
\]
For $d\in C$, the flavor fiber is
\[
\F_d=\{(c,o)\in C\times O:F(c,o)=d\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Fiber Equivalence Theorem]
For every $d\in C$, the fiber $\F_d$ is naturally in bijection with $O$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Send $(c,o)\in\F_d$ to $o$. Conversely, send $o\in O$ to $(d,o)$. The second map followed by the first is plainly the identity. Membership in the fiber gives $c=d$, so the first map followed by the second sends $(c,o)$ to $(d,o)=(c,o)$. Thus the maps are mutually inverse.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[One binary option]
If $O=\{0,1\}$, every flavor fiber contains exactly two recipes.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The fiber is in bijection with a two-element set.
\end{proof}

A cookie model may interpret the two optional states as ``without nuts'' and ``with nuts,'' provided the chosen core measurement assigns both to the same $d$.

\begin{corollary}[Independent binary options]
If $O=\{0,1\}^n$, then $|\F_d|=2^n$ for every $d\in C$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
There are two independent values at each of $n$ coordinates, hence $2^n$ assignments, and the Fiber Equivalence Theorem transfers this count to the fiber.
\end{proof}

\section{The Boolean recipe cube}

Fix $n\geq0$ and write
\[
\Q_n=\{0,1\}^n.
\]
We regard $\Q_n$ as the vertices of an $n$-cube. Coordinate $i$ records one optional binary choice. Boolean exclusive-or is denoted by $\oplus$.

\begin{definition}[Coordinate toggle]
For $i\in\{1,\ldots,n\}$, define $T_i:\Q_n\to\Q_n$ by
\[
(T_i r)_j=\begin{cases}1-r_j,&j=i,\\r_j,&j\ne i.\end{cases}
\]
Equivalently, $(T_i r)_j=r_j\oplus[j=i]$, where the bracket is $1$ if its statement is true and $0$ otherwise.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Commutativity]
For all $i,j$ and $r$, $T_i(T_j(r))=T_j(T_i(r))$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
At coordinate $k$, both sides are $r_k\oplus[k=j]\oplus[k=i]$, up to reassociation and reordering. Exclusive-or is associative and commutative.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Involutivity]
For every $i$ and $r$, $T_i(T_i(r))=r$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
At coordinate $j$, the result is $r_j\oplus[j=i]\oplus[j=i]=r_j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Method and execution]
A substitution method is a finite list $p=[i_1,\ldots,i_k]$ of coordinates. It is executed from left to right:
\[
\follow(r,[])=r,
\]
\[
\follow(r,[i_1,\ldots,i_k])=
\follow(T_{i_1}(r),[i_2,\ldots,i_k]).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Concatenation law]
For methods $p,q$,
\[
\follow(r,p\concat q)=\follow(\follow(r,p),q).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induct on $p$. The empty case is the definition. For a list beginning with $i$, execute $T_i$ and apply the induction hypothesis to the tail.
\end{proof}

\section{Parity and endpoint classification}

\begin{definition}[Parity signature]
The signature of $p=[i_1,\ldots,i_k]$ is $\sigma(p)\in\Q_n$, where
\[
\sigma(p)_j=\#\{t:i_t=j\}\pmod 2.
\]
Thus its $j$th bit records whether coordinate $j$ occurs an odd number of times.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Endpoint Formula]
For every $r\in\Q_n$, method $p$, and coordinate $j$,
\[
\follow(r,p)_j=r_j\oplus\sigma(p)_j.
\]
Equivalently, $\follow(r,p)=r\oplus\sigma(p)$ coordinatewise.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induct on the list $p$. The empty method has zero signature and fixes $r$. Suppose the method begins with $i$ and has tail $p'$. By the induction hypothesis,
\[
\follow(T_i(r),p')_j=(T_i(r))_j\oplus\sigma(p')_j.
\]
The toggle formula changes this to
\[
r_j\oplus[j=i]\oplus\sigma(p')_j.
\]
Prefixing $p'$ by $i$ toggles the $i$th signature bit, so
\[
\sigma([i]\concat p')_j=[j=i]\oplus\sigma(p')_j.
\]
Associativity of exclusive-or gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Endpoint Classification Theorem]
Fix $r\in\Q_n$. For methods $p,q$,
\[
\follow(r,p)=\follow(r,q)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\sigma(p)=\sigma(q).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Equal signatures give equal endpoints by the Endpoint Formula. Conversely, equality of endpoints gives, at each coordinate $j$,
\[
r_j\oplus\sigma(p)_j=r_j\oplus\sigma(q)_j.
\]
Canceling the common Boolean term $r_j$ yields equality of the signature bits. Hence the vectors agree.
\end{proof}

The signature is therefore a complete endpoint invariant, not merely a necessary condition. An arbitrarily long history is compressed into $n$ parity bits.

\section{Cubical path laws}

\begin{theorem}[Substitution Square]
For every state $r$ and coordinates $i,j$,
\[
\follow(r,[i,j])=\follow(r,[j,i]).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The two sides are $T_j(T_i(r))$ and $T_i(T_j(r))$, equal by commutativity. When $i\ne j$, the methods trace the two routes around a square from one vertex to the opposite vertex.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Backtracking Law]
For every $r$ and $i$, $\follow(r,[i,i])=r$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
This is the involutivity identity $T_i(T_i(r))=r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Loop]
A method $p$ is a loop based at $r$ when $\follow(r,p)=r$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Zero-Signature Loop Criterion]
A method $p$ is a loop at $r$ if and only if $\sigma(p)=0$. Equivalently, every coordinate occurs an even number of times.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Compare $p$ with the empty method in the Endpoint Classification Theorem. The empty method fixes $r$ and has zero signature.
\end{proof}

In particular, loophood in the full cube is independent of the base recipe.

\begin{theorem}[Method-Reversal Theorem]
Let $p^{\rev}$ be the list $p$ in reverse order. Then
\[
\follow(r,p\concat p^{\rev})=r.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every occurrence in $p$ occurs once more in $p^{\rev}$, so every coordinate has even total multiplicity. The concatenated signature is zero, and the Loop Criterion applies. Equivalently, reverse execution cancels the original toggles from last to first.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms and complexity}

The signature algorithm initializes an $n$-bit zero vector and scans a method $p$ of length $k$, flipping the bit indexed by each entry. It runs in $O(k)$ time and uses $O(n)$ explicit storage. The endpoint is then obtained by exclusive-or with $r$, requiring $O(n)$ additional time.

Two methods $p$ and $q$ have the same endpoint from any fixed state precisely when their signatures agree. Computing and comparing these signatures costs $O(|p|+|q|+n)$ time. A loop test computes one signature and checks whether it is zero, taking $O(k+n)$ time.

\begin{proposition}[Shortest parity representative]
List, in increasing order and exactly once, the coordinates on which $\sigma(p)$ equals $1$. This method has the same endpoint action as $p$ and has minimum length among all methods with that action.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The proposed list has signature $\sigma(p)$, so the Endpoint Classification Theorem gives the same action. Any method with this signature must mention each coordinate carrying a $1$ at least once. Its length is therefore at least the Hamming weight of $\sigma(p)$, which the proposed list attains.
\end{proof}

The signature is reusable: once computed, it predicts the endpoint from every possible starting recipe. It also supports deduplication of transformation plans and generation of short explanations of net ingredient changes.

\section{Topological interpretation}

A one-choice fiber contains two states. If no substitution edge is included, its geometric realization is two isolated points, homeomorphic to the zero-sphere $S^0$. If the toggle edge is included, however, the two vertices and edge form an interval, which is contractible. The state set alone does not determine topology; adjacency and higher cells must be declared.

For $n$ independent choices, all single-coordinate toggles give the one-skeleton of an $n$-cube. Filling a square for every pair of independent toggles, and higher cells for larger independent sets, gives the ordinary cube $[0,1]^n$. Its realization is contractible. The Substitution Square supplies the elementary endpoint equality around each square, but a full path-homotopy theory additionally declares those squares to be filled.

Nontrivial topology can arise from constraints. Let $A\subseteq\Q_n$ encode admissible recipes subject to allergies, availability, compatibility, cost, or flavor preservation. Retain an edge only if both endpoints are admissible. The resulting induced subspace can be disconnected or contain unfilled cycles. Such features belong to the specified constrained model, not to the unconstrained Boolean set by itself.

A claim that a cyclic culinary process has fundamental group $\mathbb Z$ requires a circle-like space: for example, a finite cycle graph with oriented stages, edge-path reduction, and a declared path homotopy. One may then assign loops an integer winding number and prove that it classifies homotopy classes. The full cube does not imply such a result, because its square and higher cells make it contractible.

\section{Applications and extensions}

In constraint-aware substitution planning, an admissibility predicate removes forbidden states and transitions. In the unconstrained system, the shortest parity representative immediately gives an optimal transformation. Under constraints, graph search can seek a legal route, while parity remains a useful endpoint summary.

For measured recipe data, one may map recipes into $\mathbb R^m$, choose a distance and tolerance $\varepsilon$, and connect sufficiently close observations or build a simplicial complex by a declared neighborhood rule. Connected components and persistent homology then become reproducible computations. A dataset alone is insufficient: the metric, threshold, and simplex rule are part of the mathematical model.

Binary coordinates can also be replaced by finite ingredient-state sets $O_i$. The optional state space becomes
\[
O=\prod_{i=1}^n O_i,
\qquad
|\F_d|=\prod_{i=1}^n|O_i|.
\]
Substitutions may then act by cyclic increments or finite permutations. Boolean parity is replaced by the algebra of cyclic or permutation groups.

Algebraically, the binary toggles generate $(\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z)^n$. This group acts on $\Q_n$ by exclusive-or, freely and transitively. The recipe cube is therefore a torsor for the signature group. Commutation, involutivity, and endpoint classification are all consequences of this action.

\section{Limitations and future work}

The model separates three levels. The fiber theorem follows from representing a recipe as core plus optional data. The parity theorems follow from the coordinate-toggle action. Topological conclusions require explicit edges and higher cells. Equality of modeled core values is not a claim of experimentally established sensory identity, and endpoint equality is not automatically path homotopy.

A natural next step is to define the full cubical set and prove directly that its realization is contractible, while selected discrete subspaces realize $S^0$. A rewriting theory should identify methods under deletion of adjacent duplicate toggles and swaps of adjacent independent toggles; the expected normal-form theorem states that two methods are equivalent exactly when their signatures agree.

Constrained predicates offer finite examples with genuine topology. Algorithms can compute connected components, shortest paths, cycle ranks, and higher features. A separate cyclic model can support a winding-number classification and a fundamental group isomorphic to $\mathbb Z$. Empirical work should specify measured flavor maps, metrics, thresholds, and complex-building rules before invoking persistent homology. Finally, multi-state choices lead naturally to products of finite spaces and actions by cyclic and permutation groups.

\section{Conclusion}

Fixed observed flavor leaves exactly the optional ingredient data. Independent binary choices create $2^n$ cubical vertices. Methods are paths generated by coordinate toggles, and parity signatures completely classify their endpoints. Independent substitutions commute, duplicate substitutions cancel, zero signatures characterize loops, and reversal retraces every method.

The framework does not assign topology to cuisine through metaphor alone. It identifies the ingredients of a precise geometric model: states, transitions, higher cells, constraints, and observation rules. Once these are explicit, recipe variation becomes a tractable meeting point of combinatorics, algebra, geometry, and computation.

\end{document}

