# Explore the Geometry of Recipe Substitutions

A recipe can be separated into an observed flavor core and optional choices. Fixing the core leaves a **fiber** of alternatives. With $n$ independent binary choices, that fiber has $2^n$ states—the vertices of an $n$-cube.

[Learn more about the hypercube](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypercube) and [exclusive-or](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exclusive_or), the two elementary structures behind the model.

## 1. Count the possibilities

Start with the exact growth law. The chart and executable example compare enumeration with $2^n$.

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the fiber argument</summary>
For a fixed flavor $d$, every recipe has the form $(d,o)$, where $o$ is its optional assignment. Forgetting $d$ gives a bijection between the fiber and the optional state space. Binary assignments on $n$ coordinates number $2^n$.
</details>

## 2. Walk through the cube

Each button below toggles one coordinate. Different orders can reach the same vertex.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the endpoint theorem</summary>
The signature $\sigma(p)$ records which coordinates occur oddly in a method $p$. Execution obeys $\operatorname{follow}(r,p)=r\oplus\sigma(p)$. Therefore two methods have the same endpoint from a fixed state exactly when their signatures agree.
</details>

## 3. Compress a long method

Use the laboratory to watch repeated substitutions cancel. The increasing list of coordinates with odd parity is a shortest equivalent method.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Why is the representative shortest?</summary>
Every coordinate whose signature bit is $1$ must be toggled at least once by any equivalent method. Listing each such coordinate once reaches the lower bound given by the signature's Hamming weight.
</details>

## 4. Squares and loops

Independent substitutions commute, so two orders trace opposite sides of a square. Repeating one toggle cancels. A method is a loop precisely when all coordinate counts are even.

{{visualization:2}}

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the reversal proof</summary>
Append a method's reversal. Every toggle then occurs twice in the combined list, so every signature coordinate is zero. The zero-signature criterion makes the combined method a loop at every starting recipe.
</details>

## 5. From cubes to culinary topology

A discrete one-choice fiber is two points, the zero-sphere $S^0$. Adding the edge between those points instead makes an interval. The full cube with all higher faces is contractible. Nontrivial topology requires constraints that remove states or cells, or a separately declared cyclic model. See introductions to [cubical complexes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubical_complex) and [persistent homology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persistent_homology) for the broader setting.

<details><summary>Modeling cautions and research frontier</summary>
Measured recipe data require an explicit flavor map, distance, threshold, and simplex rule. An integer winding number requires a genuine cycle and a declared path equivalence. Future work includes constrained subcomplexes, path-rewriting normal forms, empirical flavor neighborhoods, and non-binary ingredient state spaces.
</details>

